From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Mon, 29 Sep 2026 05:50:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Engine: a question Tiger's CoreText cannot be asked

Font::canRenderCombiningCharacterSequence hands the sequence to
CTLineCreateWithUniCharProvider and reads back which font CoreText chose.
On 10.4 that call dereferences null inside TFontCascade::CreateFallback
when it meets a character no installed font maps.

Found by a site survey rather than by looking: 68kmla.org took the browser
down on a PowerBook G3 running 10.4.11, and did it again on demand -
EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x00000000 both times, from
ComplexTextController::collectComplexTextRuns through
fontForCombiningCharacterSequence into CoreText. The survey had called it
"exited without a crash report", because Tiger appends to one
CaptainPolliwog.crash.log where Leopard writes a file per crash, and the
harness was looking for a new file.

There is nothing to guard on our side: the crash is inside the create call,
not in what it returns, and a null check on the result would never be
reached. So on systems older than 10.5 the question is not asked and the
font is taken at its word.

That is a real loss and worth stating: a combining sequence the font cannot
actually draw is now drawn by it anyway, without its marks, instead of being
handed to a font that could. Leopard and later are unchanged and still ask.
A worse glyph on Tiger, and a browser that is still running.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
  Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/cocoa/FontCocoa.mm | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/cocoa/FontCocoa.mm
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/cocoa/FontCocoa.mm
@@ -39,6 +39,7 @@
 #import "SharedBuffer.h"
 #import "WebCoreSystemInterface.h"
 #import <float.h>
+#import <sys/sysctl.h>
 #import <unicode/uchar.h>
 #import <wtf/Assertions.h>
 #import <wtf/RetainPtr.h>
@@ -728,6 +729,36 @@
     return info->characters + stringIndex;
 }
 
+// Tiger cannot be asked this question without falling over.
+//
+// The check below hands the sequence to CTLineCreateWithUniCharProvider and
+// reads back which font CoreText chose. On 10.4 that call dereferences null
+// inside TFontCascade::CreateFallback when it meets a character no installed
+// font maps - reproduced on a PowerBook G3 running 10.4.11, twice, loading
+// 68kmla.org: EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x00000000 both times, browser gone, no page.
+//
+// There is nothing to guard on our side: the crash is inside the create call,
+// not in what it returns. So on systems older than 10.5 the question is not
+// asked and the font is taken at its word. The cost is that a combining
+// sequence this font cannot really draw is drawn by it anyway, without its
+// marks, instead of being handed to another font. That is a worse glyph and
+// a live browser.
+static bool coreTextCanBeAskedAboutCombiningSequences()
+{
+    static bool checked = false;
+    static bool canBeAsked = true;
+
+    if (!checked) {
+        char release[32] = { 0 };
+        size_t size = sizeof release;
+        // Darwin 8 is 10.4, Darwin 9 is 10.5.
+        if (sysctlbyname("kern.osrelease", release, &size, 0, 0) == 0)
+            canBeAsked = atoi(release) >= 9;
+        checked = true;
+    }
+    return canBeAsked;
+}
+
 bool Font::canRenderCombiningCharacterSequence(const UChar* characters, size_t length) const
 {
     ASSERT(isMainThread());
@@ -739,6 +770,11 @@
     if (!addResult.isNewEntry)
         return addResult.iterator->value;
 
+    if (!coreTextCanBeAskedAboutCombiningSequences()) {
+        addResult.iterator->value = true;
+        return true;
+    }
+
     RetainPtr<CFTypeRef> fontEqualityObject = platformData().objectForEqualityCheck();
 
     ProviderInfo info = { characters, length, getCFStringAttributes(false, platformData().orientation()) };
-- 
2.39.5

